1 Introduction
The prediction of rotor noise radiation is important for helicopter certification and operation [1]. Especially in descending flight, blade–vortex interaction (BVI) noise is generated by the main rotor blades in the first and fourth quadrant of the rotor disk. There, the blade leading edge and the blade tip vortices of preceding blades (or of the blade of investigation) are almost parallel to each other, causing a high frequency surface pressure variation simultaneously over a large part of the rotor blade. The major parameter of noise radiation—besides the vortex circulation strength, blade Mach number and blade loading during the interaction—is the vertical blade–vortex missdistance [2]. Thus, the prediction of this missdistance is of utmost importance when computing BVI noise. While the computation of blade air loads and the blade dynamic response associated with these is state of the art and can be reliably performed with comprehensive codes, the prediction of the wake geometry is still a challenging task and was not possible at all until about 1960. In the 1960s, computers became available and made possible the application of computationally intensive methods to engineering problems. Since then, computer power has quickly developed and with it the possibility to apply more and more complex algorithms, opening the door for BVI noise prediction using different levels of sophistication. A good overview of available engineering methods of rotor wake prediction and their application is given in [3]. Essentially, three methods are applied today for computation of BVI noise radiation. First are prescribed wake codes, where the wake geometry is purely defined by a few parameters, namely the thrust coefficient, the advance ratio, the climb or descent velocity, and the tip path plane angle of attack. Based on the experimental data in hover obtained in 1956 [4], the first simple prescribed wake geometry model was
developed. More recognized is the experimental work and math modeling of Landgrebe [5] in 1971 and later. Once the wake geometry is computed, it is held constant, independent of how the azimuthal loading distribution of a rotor blade may vary during its revolution. The major advantage is that only the induced velocities of all vortices of the wake at the aerodynamic point of interest of the revolving blade need to be computed, which is associated with very moderate computational amount. Probably, the latest development was given by Beddoes [6] that included a simple approximation to the lateral roll-up of the wake system. The major shortcomings of classical prescribed wake codes are that they include no response of the wake geometry to load variations within the disk (for example, as caused by means of higher harmonic or individual blade control) for otherwise constant steady operating conditions of the rotor. These load variations have been demonstrated in wind tunnel and flight tests to be the major source of noise reduction. Second are free-wake codes, which can be represented by a vortex lattice method. Therein, at any instant of time the differential equations of motion of every individual vortex element within the entire wake is solved. Thus, the shortcomings of classical prescribed wake codes are not present in free-wake codes. This has resulted in the rotorcraft community assuming that the usage of free-wake codes is mandatory when active rotor control is to be investigated, especially for BVI noise radiation. This requires the computation of induced velocities of all vortices in the wake on all lattice nodes. This is a tremendous effort, considering a representative wake of a four-bladed rotor consists of about NbNwNrev = 4 9 72 9 5 = 1440 vortex segments only for the tip vortices, even with truncation after the fifth wake revolution. With all inboard wake vortex filaments, this number is considerably higher since this includes NR ? 1 trailed vortices and NRNw shed vortices, both per blade. It is immediately apparent that this is associated with a tremendous increase of computing effort compared to the prescribed wake approach, but the method is inherently capable of responding to any kind of blade loading distribution. Consequently, the resulting wake geometry can be expected to be much more accurate, and this is the reason why the majority of BVI noise prediction attempts are based on free-wake codes. The application of such codes started by Scully [7] and was increasingly used and developed since then by numerous authors; the latest developments have been generated by Leishman et al. [8]. The third method is the use of computational fluid dynamics (CFD) codes, which attempt to solve the fluid differential equations of motion (Navier–Stokes or Euler formulation) in space and time. This is performed by discretizing the entire volume of space around the rotor into millions of cells and solving these equations numerically
