As an example, a four-bladed rotor Nb = 4 may be discretized into NR = 20 blade elements (this is the lower end for acoustic investigations; a fine resolution would have 40–50 elements). If the wake elements may have a length of 10° azimuth (about the coarsest resolution, i.e., Nw = 36, a fine resolution would have elements no longer than 5°, i.e. 72 per revolution) and the wake may extend over only Nrev = 2 revolutions in length (this covers just the rotor disk as minimum requirement; a usual length would be 4–5 revolutions). Then, the above ratio amounts approximately to 1:4 9 105. If only the tip vortex is retained and all other trailed and shed vortices are ignored
(with the reasoning that the tip vortex strength is about 10 times larger than all others), then this reduces to approximately 1:2 9 104. Including all kinds of acceleration measures (for example: computing the wake–wake interaction only for vortex elements within a certain distance to the node of interest), this can be further reduced to about 1:103, while these numbers are based on the minimal discretization. As a rough estimate, the relation of computational effort can thus be assumed very conservatively as 1:103:106 for prescribed wake:free-wake:CFD. Therefore, prescribed and free-wake codes are favourable for routine application and the free-wake approach was seen as the best compromise of computational effort and accuracy obtainable. However, it appears that the potential advantages of prescribed wake codes are not fully exploited today. As will be shown in this paper, the wake geometry modifications due to azimuthal loading variation of the rotor blades, as well as the effect of fuselage displacement flow on the wake geometry can be accounted for in a prescribed wake to a first order of accuracy. This extends the usage of prescribed wakes significantly, especially when a large number of parameter variations is to be investigated with respect to its effect of BVI noise radiation. An example of a free-wake result is given in Fig. 1. Although the fuselage is shown below the rotor, its effect on the wake distortion was neglected and the results are based on an isolated rotor. Yet, some fundamental general behaviour can already be identified qualitatively:
• In the front of the disk, the vortices are convected upwards above the disk. Towards the rear and behind, a strong downwash is present in the middle of the first and fourth quadrant, indicating a strong longitudinal gradient of the induced velocities. • In the middle of the disk and behind it, in a lateral extension of about the root cut-out area, the vortices remain above the disk before being convected downwards further behind the disk. • On the right and left sides of the disk, the tip vortices bundle around each other and remain above the disk as if an upwash is present there. This is the roll-up region, and far behind the rotor this appears very similar to the wake of a fixed wing aircraft with two long straight tip vortices.
Within a comprehensive rotor simulation environment, all of these effects must be addressed appropriately to at least a first order of accuracy. In addition, the proximity of the fuselage to the rotor disk should also be taken into account, which currently is not done.
