2 Fundamentals of prescribed wake computation
The prediction of the wake geometry requires the knowledge of the flow field wherein the vortex elements are convected downstream (mainly due to flight speed), in vertical direction (mainly due to self-induced velocities, but also due to fuselage flow displacement) and in lateral direction (to a lesser extent, due to the same reasons). The only known parameters are the flight velocities in all directions and the rotor disk attitude. However, the latter already implies some inaccuracy since the rotor blades are flexible, and the actual shape of the tip path plane is deformed rather than planar. Mostly unknown are the selfinduced velocities, which in reality are extremely complex and non-linear with respect to both the radial and azimuthal directions. Therefore, several simplifying assumptions must be established in order to allow a wake geometry to be computed analytically, based on a few available parameters. First, the induced velocities are assumed to be independent of the vertical coordinate. For the moderate vertical deflections of the wake geometry this is considered not to be an acceptable simplification since the self-induced velocities originate from the wake itself. Thus, whatever deflection the wake undergoes, the relative position of one vortex element to another does not show large variation. Second, the non-uniformity of the induced velocities may be replaced by a simple linear variation in the longitudinal and lateral direction based on elementary momentum theory considerations. This is a strong simplification and thus most questionable. However, high frequency variations of induced velocities (which is equivalent to local spatial variations) that may be present along the convection path of a vortex element will be passed quickly
and thus does not cause large wake displacements. Of more concern are low frequency velocity field oscillations (or, in spatial terms: long wave length distributions) since they affect the vortex path for a longer time and will cause larger and more significant deflections. Therefore, it is important to consider this low frequency content of induced velocities when computing the prescribed wake geometry.
2.1 Rotor-induced velocity field
The most simple assumption regarding the induced velocity distribution within the rotor disk is to apply momentum theory to the rotor thrust for the operational condition under consideration. The fundamentals of momentum theory were developed by Rankine [10] and Froude [11], and the application to rotary-wing problems first was performed by Glauert [12], then by Lock [13]. Glauert already suggested a linear longitudinal variation of induced velocities, which was then applied by Lock. A lateral gradient was introduced by Drees [14]. Thus, a simple induced velocity field within the rotor disk may be written in the form
