2.4 Induced velocity field behind the rotor
For computing the wake geometry, not only the velocity field inside the rotor disk is needed but also the velocity field behind it. Since the further deformations behind the disk are of no importance for the BVI problem inside the disk, more rigorous assumptions as to the distribution of induced velocities are allowed. The most simple one is to assume a constant mean induced velocity everywhere without any further gradients. For the longitudinal gradient, this is justified since at any location behind the disk the part of wake generating significant influence on a vortex filament is as long ahead of it as behind of it. This symmetry precludes a gradient of induced velocity in x direction. A lateral gradient may still exist due to different vortex circulation strengths on the advancing and retreating side, following the result of Drees [14]. The same applies for the roll-up due to Beddoes [6]; this process is initiated within the disk and continues to develop in the wake behind. Another way of examining the problem may again be taken from momentum theory. Far from the rotor (in hover: underneath it; in forward flight: behind it), the induced slipstream is fully developed and has twice the speed it had within the rotor, i.e. ki = 2ki0. In order to ease the analysis, this result may be evaluated at the rear of the rotor disk and
applied everywhere downstream. As a result, the induced velocity field in the wake behind the rotor disk is
kiðx; yÞ 1⁄4 2ki0 k0 kxjyj3 þ kyy ð9Þ
with k0 as defined in Eq. 7. As an example, in Fig. 4 the induced velocity distributions within the rotor disk of the four models of Glauert, Lock, Drees and Beddoes are given. For convenience, the induced flow has been referenced to the mean value, and the scale is denoted by kn/k0, which in this case is ki(x, y)/ki0.
2.5 Operating condition
The reference operating condition used throughout the paper is the HART II baseline (BL) case [24]. The HART II wind tunnel test was performed in 2001 in the 8 m 9 6 m open-jet configuration of the large low-speed facility of the DNW by an international team of DLR, Onera, NASA Langley, US Army AFDD and DNW. A 40% Mach scaled hingeless Bo105 model rotor was used to explore the effect of 3/rev HHC on the wake geometry and the noise radiation. A rotor blade was equipped with absolute pressure sensors along the leading edge for detection of BVI locations and at one radial position for analysis of the section airloads. The BL case is defined by an advance ratio of l = 0.15, a thrust coefficient of CT = 0.0044 and a tip path plane angle of attack of a = 4.5°, including wind tunnel corrections. This represents a descending flight where strong BVI occurs, i.e., BVI takes place around w = 50° and w = 300° within the rotor disk. There, the vortex axis and the blade leading edge are approximately parallel to each other. In addition to the BL case, two cases with 3/rev HHC were investigated: one with a 3/rev control phase of 300° that was found to generate minimum noise radiation (MN) and another one with a control phase of 180° for minimum vibration generation (MV). A large portion of the HART II data including all documentation was made available for the HART II International Workshop and is accessible for download from NASA (http://rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov/hart2/) or DLR (ftp://HART-II@ftp.dlr.de/). The data have been widely used in the community for code validation.
2.6 Computation of the wake geometry
Any vortex element of the wake was released at the trailing edge of its generating blade. In most cases this will be at the blade tip, but, depending on the radial distribution of blade loading, this may also be at a more inboard location. In cases where the blade tip region experiences a download and the inner part of the blade an upload two vortices will be released: one at the tip with opposite sense of rotation
