due to the download and another one inboard where the radial gradient of bound circulation is maximum. This inboard vortex will have the conventional sense of rotation. Since a download in the tip region usually is present only over a part of the revolution such dual vortex systems are developing only in these regions and everywhere else a single tip vortex is existing. For every azimuthal position of the blade, the tip vortex release point (and that of the secondary vortex, if present) is defined by the azimuth of the generating blade wb, the radial release position rvr, and the trailing edge position vertically with respect the hub plane, zte, which is given by the blade flapping position of this radius, and by the total local blade pitch due to the blade pre-twist, the control angle (including active control, if applied) and the elastic torsion. Thus, the coordinates of the vortex release point are
xvr 1⁄4 rvr cos wb; yvr 1⁄4 rvr sin wb; zvr 1⁄4 zte ð10Þ
From this point, the vortex convects downstream along x by the in-plane component of the oncoming velocity V1 cos a or, in non-dimensional form: the advance ratio l. It convects vertically depending on the distribution of vertical velocities along its downstream convection, that are the induced
velocities vi and the out-of-plane component of the oncoming velocity V1 sin a or, in non-dimensional form: ki and lz. Since disturbances of the flow field are mainly in the vertical direction and less in the downstream and lateral directions, the focus here is placed only on the vertical convection and associated vortex trajectories. In general, disturbances in any direction could be handled the same way, but this is left for future investigations. Any vortices generated at the front of the disk, i.e., between p/2 \ wb \ 3p/2, have to pass through the rotor disk and the induced velocity field therein, while all elements generated at the rear of the disk, i.e., between 3p/2 \ wb \ 2p and 0 \ wb \ p/2, are always behind the rotor disk and are only affected by the induced velocities there. The vertical velocity field through which a vortex element has to pass consists of two contributions. First, resulting from the inclination of the tip path plane with respect to the flight direction, lz 1⁄4 l tan a is constant across and behind the disk. Second, the induced velocity ki(x, y) acts normal to the tip path plane, which, since the inclination with respect to the hub plane is usually small, can be assumed to be normal to the hub plane. Thus, the total velocity normal to the hub plane is given by k(x, y) = ki(x, y) ? lz.
