3 Extension to harmonic blade loading
As the steady thrust defines the mean induced flow by means of momentum theory it must be assumed as well that the azimuthally (and radially) varying distribution of the blade lift will cause an associated amount of varying induced flow. This is in analogy of the combined blade element/momentum theory, which is elaborated in most textbooks to the subject, like in [15, 16]. In the examples given therein it is applied to the hover case with constant azimuthal (i.e., rotationally symmetric) yet radially varying distribution of the blade’s lift distribution. The momentum theory then is applied to each of the blade elements individually and independently, assuming that there is no interference between the elements. In analogy and with the same justification to this radial variation of induced flow there can be assumed the same for the azimuthally varying part of the blade lift. In the case of constant operating conditions (for example: in forward flight) a rotor blade will—after all transients
