4 Accounting for the fuselage
The flow around the fuselage causes disturbances within the rotor disk since the fuselage and rotor usually are close to each other. Fuselage proximity is commonly acknowledged to be a prime cause of rotor vibration, but a large rotor-fuselage separation distance requires a long rotor shaft (thus increasing the empty mass of the vehicle) and simultaneously may cause problems with respect to ground resonance due to a reduced stiffness of the hub attachment to the fuselage. However, the effect of flow induced by the fuselage on rotor blade aerodynamics often is ignored in comprehensive simulation codes and even more rarely included in prescribed and free-wake models. To account for the fuselage displacement flow, either a panel method or CFD code must be applied in order to obtain the distribution of fuselage-induced velocities on the rotor disk. In general, the flow field is fully three-dimensional with large gradients in all directions close to the fuselage, and a non-linear velocity distribution will be obtained in the rotor disk. In a cylinder of a few chords in length above and below the rotor disk such gradients will still prevail, but it can be assumed that the gradients in longitudinal (x) and lateral (y) are much larger than in the vertical (z) direction, since within this cylinder the extension in x and y is much larger than in z. Therefore, to simplify the problem and to reduce it to the fundamental underlying physics, an assumption can be made that within this cylinder (wherein all the vortex filaments are traveling downstream) no gradients in z direction are present and only the variation of fuselage-induced velocities normal to the disk are taken into account. However, the validity of this assumption must be verified by computing the velocity field in the volume of interest and analyzing it for the gradients.
4.1 Fuselage-induced velocities
In the incompressible case, the fuselage-induced velocities are linearly proportional to the flight speed, which is in
contrast to the rotor-induced velocities caused by rotor thrust, which based on momentum theory approach zero for very high speed. Since helicopters do not generally fly faster than a Mach number of 0.3, the assumption of incompressible flow over the fuselage is valid. Therefore, the application of an incompressible panel method is justified to approximate the fuselage-induced velocities that will impinge and influence the rotor disk. This has been performed in [20]. The result was formulated in terms of polynomials of third order in radial direction and a Fourier series up to the seventh harmonic in azimuthal direction for convenient usage in a comprehensive rotor simulation environment. An example of the flow induced by the wind tunnel model fuselage is given in Fig. 10. Only cos nw terms appear due to the lateral symmetry of the fuselage. Yet, this model has not been used so far for affecting the prescribed wake’s geometry, which is addressed in this section. The normal velocities in the rotor plane (z = 0) of two different fuselages are shown: in Fig. 10a a fuselage as used on a vertical strut in the wind tunnel is modeled by a panel method, and in (b) its induced velocity field in the plane of the rotor disk at z = 0 is shown. The fuselage used in the HART II test mounted on the rear sting support is shown in Fig. 1 and is computed by a NavierStokes code. In (c), the fuselage-induced velocities of this HART II fuselage are shown for comparison to (b) and in (d) the velocities along the centreline at y = 0 are given for different heights above the rotor disk with increments of 10% of the radius (=1.65 chord lengths). Again the data are extracted from a Navier–Stokes computation. As can be seen, there is a strong effect of distance to the fuselage, and a generic model should include these. For small variances around z = 0, however, this effect may be ignored. Since the fuselage-induced flow is described similar to the induced velocities due to harmonic blade load