 the blade motion of the S4 results. A wide range of validation cases has been performed with respect to blade motion (in terms of the static and dynamic tip deflection in flap and torsion), section loading at 87% radius with respect to the lower harmonics and especially the high frequency content caused by bladevortex interaction (BVI). The qualitative location of most intense BVI within the rotor disk, the vortex trajectory at two lateral positions of y = ±0.7, and finally the noise radiation are also available. As an example, the application of the wake improvements is demonstrated to assess the difference between the HART II baseline case (BL) and the minimum noise case (MN) was presented in [22]. The operational condition is the same for both cases as noted before. In the BL case no HHC input was applied, while in the MN case a 3/rev HHC with a blade root control amplitude of 0.8° and a phase of
300° was introduced. A 3/rev air load variation occurred naturally on the blade, with some increase of the 2/rev and the 4/rev due to inter-harmonic coupling in forward flight. In Fig. 12 the leading edge pressure distribution for these cases is given and is high-pass filtered at 10/rev in order to make the blade-vortex interaction (BVI) locations visible. Comparing the MN case Fig. 12b to the BL case (a), the BVI locations in the first quadrant of the disk have moved inboard and towards the front of the disk, where the interaction angle is far from parallel, thus reducing noise. The location in the baseline case at about 50° azimuth, where parallel BVI occurred, is free of BVI in the MN case. This is caused by a larger blade lift (due to HHC and the associated blade response in torsion and flapping) in the second quadrant, generating more downwash in this region than in the BL case. Tip vortices traveling across this downwash are convected downward and thus pass through the rotor disk further upstream than in the BL case. On the retreating side, the BVI locations also have moved towards the front of the disk, again due to a larger blade loading around 230° azimuth, caused by HHC and the blade aeroelastic response. The simulation of these two cases is given in Fig. 13 in form of the blade loading CnM2, which is again high-pass filtered at 10/rev, allowing a qualitative comparison of BVI locations and relative strength with the experimental data of Fig. 12. First, the Beddoes’ model (including the correction for the mean downwash) is applied to the BL case, Fig. 13a. A good overall agreement can be seen with the experiment for both the advancing and the retreating side BVI. Since the operating condition (trim) of the rotor was unchanged when HHC was applied in the MN case, the Beddoes’ model does not change its geometry, see Fig. 13b, except for the blade motion, which is different from the BL case and cannot directly be seen in the figure. However, the flapping is very limited due to centrifugal forces. The vortex circulation strength changes significantly according to the blade air loads. As a consequence, the BVI locations remain virtually unchanged, but the strength at the interaction location has increased due to the strong vortex circulation caused by HHC. Comparing Fig. 13a with (b), an increase of noise radiation is the immediate consequence, but in direct opposition to what the experiment has shown. This effect has caused the rotorcraft community to judge prescribed wake codes as inappropriate for active control conditions. Next, the application of the wake deflections due to harmonic blade loading results in the BVI locations shown in Fig. 13c. Compared to the BL case in (a), the BVI locations have shifted significantly inboard in the first quadrant and towards the front of the disk. This is in good qualitative and—with respect to the geometry—quantitative agreement with the experiment shown in Fig. 12b. On the retreating side, the BVI
