7 Conclusions
In this paper, several models have been presented that may be applied to any existing basic prescribed wake geometry. These models account for:
1. blade root cut-out in the centre of the hub, 2. harmonic inflow due to harmonic blade loading and the associated wake distortions, 3. stationary inflow due to the presence of the fuselage and the associated wake distortions.
It is found that the root cut-out results in an increased downwash in the remaining part of the disk due to momentum theory. The most visible effect is found behind and downstream of the rotor centre, but it does not affect the locations of blade-vortex interaction in the first and fourth quadrants. A major and very important contribution results from the new harmonic inflow model and the wake distortion caused by it. This is a key parameter for a reliable computation of BVI locations in the rotor disk, and the noise radiation generated. The application of only the fundamental basic wake geometry cannot predict the BVI locations correctly since it does not modify the wake geometry. The inclusion of the fuselage-induced flow field and the associated wake distortion is mainly found along the centreline of the rotor disk and to the right and left of it, while it quickly reduces when moving more far away from the fuselage in the lateral direction. Thus, its effect on BVI location is not large, but further validation remains to be done. It represents essential physics in a plausible manner and should be taken into account in future. All of these models are able to be superimposed to each other and to any available prescribed basic wake model. This allows them to be added without modification of the existing applied wake. All wake distortions due to harmonic loading and fuselage directly are additive. This modularity appears as a major advantage. In total, with the addition of these models the prescribed wake codes are significantly improved and their applicability enhanced. The remaining gap in accuracy compared to free-wake codes is recouped by the significant amount of
computation time saved when using prescribed wake codes compared to free-wake codes.
